From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:36:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: s2r: name the resume step and driver a
 panic came from

A deep sleep split at depth 7 on T8132 resets with the PMU boot stage
byte still at 0x39 (syscore resume done): the stop lies between syscore
resume and ->finish (0x3a), in the interrupt enable, the wake report or
the noirq and early device resume. With kernel.panic 5 the reset came
1..6 s after the call, with kernel.panic 0 at the syscore resume's own
120 s watchdog: a panic. Nothing on the next boot says which step or
which driver, since the memory and the log are gone with the reset.

Add resume_trail, a bring-up parameter (default off) that only acts
with the breadcrumb on. From syscore resume on:

 - 0x36 is written at ->wake, 0x37 at the first noirq and 0x38 at the
   first early device resume callback. The boot firmware counts these
   0x3X values with a reset, and the T8132 sequence never writes them
   before 0x39. The driver core's device_pm_callback_start tracepoint
   gives the callbacks; its probe is registered at ->begin and removed
   at ->end, so nothing runs outside a traced sleep.
 - A panic or an oops from there to ->end arms the watchdog, which this
   driver holds through the resume, again with a time that names the
   driver of the last device callback: 12 s none yet, then 8 s apart
   for DART, PMGR power state, PCIe, NVMe, ATC PHY, pinctrl and any
   other. The panic is kept from restarting before that time.

The phase byte and the reset time together name the step. A host test
checks the phase selection, the class of each driver name (and that
each name is that driver's own), and the time table.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
